    "body": "## Bug\n\nThe `UpdateQuoteTtl` gRPC method (defined in `management.proto` L177-183) accepts separate `mint_ttl` and `melt_ttl` fields, implying per-quote-type expiry control. However, the implementation in `management_rpc.py` L105-113 has two issues:\n\n### 1. Wrong setting updated\nBoth fields write to `settings.mint_redis_cache_ttl`, which controls **Redis response cache duration** — not quote expiration. Quote expiry is actually derived from the Bolt11 invoice's own expiry timestamp at creation time and is unaffected by this RPC call.\n\n```python\nasync def UpdateQuoteTtl(self, request, context):\n    if request.mint_ttl:\n        settings.mint_redis_cache_ttl = request.mint_ttl\n    elif request.melt_ttl:\n        settings.mint_redis_cache_ttl = request.melt_ttl\n```\n\n### 2. Both fields map to the same single setting\n`mint_ttl` and `melt_ttl` both overwrite `settings.mint_redis_cache_ttl`. There is no way to set different values for mint vs melt. Additionally, due to `elif`, if both fields are provided in a single request, `melt_ttl` is silently ignored.\n\n### Expected behavior\n`UpdateQuoteTtl` should control how long mint/melt quotes remain valid (i.e., override or supplement the Bolt11-derived expiry), with independent settings for each quote type.\n\n### Actual behavior\nThe method silently changes the Redis cache TTL instead, with no effect on quote expiration.\n",
